.Dd $Mdocdate: November 11 2026 $
.Dt AIMAP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aimap
.Nd Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dnVv
.Op Fl D Ar macro Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an Internet Message Access Protocol
.Pq IMAP
daemon implementing a subset of IMAP4rev2, as defined by RFC 9051,
and the RFC 7162 CONDSTORE and QRESYNC extensions.
It serves mail stored in maildir format, delivered by
.Xr smtpd 8
as described in
.Xr aimap.conf 5 ,
and rewrites each new message with the CRLF line endings RFC 5322
requires before any client sees it.
.Pp
Message files that other software places in a mailbox's
.Pa cur
or
.Pa new
directory, such as a maildir brought from another server, are given
UIDs the next time the mailbox is selected or its status is read,
keeping the flags in their names.
A mailbox whose
.Pa aimap.index
is lost is rebuilt the same way, with a new
.Li UIDVALIDITY .
A file found in
.Pa cur
with the deleted flag
.Pq Sq T
is left out unless the mailbox has no index, since it may be a
message that was expunged but could not be removed.
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
listens on port 143, where clients may upgrade to TLS with
.Li STARTTLS ,
and on port 993 with implicit TLS, as recommended by RFC 8314.
Authentication is
.Li AUTH=PLAIN
only, and is refused until TLS is established.
Users are not system accounts; they are listed in the credentials and
userbase files described in
.Xr aimap.conf 5 .
.Pp
Each user's mailboxes form a single flat namespace:
.Li INBOX
is the account's maildir, and each other mailbox is a maildir in a
subdirectory of it with the mailbox's name.
.Pp
.Nm
requires the
.Sy _aimap
and
.Sy _aimapauth
users to exist, and
.Pa /var/empty
to be a directory owned by root and writable by neither group nor other.
It logs to
.Xr syslogd 8
with the
.Dv LOG_MAIL
facility and rereads its configuration file on
.Dv SIGHUP .
A reload that brings a new TLS private key closes every session.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl D Ar macro Ns = Ns Ar value
Define
.Ar macro
to be set to
.Ar value
on the command line.
Overrides the definition of
.Ar macro
in the configuration file.
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize.
If this option is specified,
.Nm
will run in the foreground and log to
.Em stderr .
Each child still runs in a process group of its own, so the terminal's
interrupt and suspend characters reach only the parent process.
When the parent exits, the children follow.
.It Fl f Ar file
Use
.Ar file
as the configuration file, instead of the default
.Pa /etc/aimap.conf .
.It Fl n
Configtest mode.
Only check the configuration file for validity, including that the
TLS certificate and key load and match.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose logging.
Multiple
.Fl v
options increase the verbosity.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/ssl/private/aimap.keyXX" -compact
.It Pa /etc/aimap.conf
Default configuration file.
.It Pa /etc/aimap/credentials
Default credentials file.
.It Pa /etc/aimap/userbase
Default userbase file.
.It Pa /etc/ssl/aimap.crt
Default TLS certificate.
.It Pa /etc/ssl/private/aimap.key
Default TLS private key.
.It Pa /var/empty
.Xr chroot 2
directory of the listener, keymgr and parser processes.
.It Pa /var/mail/aimap
Default mail spool.
.It Pa aimap.index
Index of a mailbox's messages, in each mailbox's maildir, with
.Pa aimap.index.lock
and
.Pa aimap.index.tmp
beside it.
.It Pa aimap.subscriptions
Subscribed mailboxes, at the root of each maildir, with
.Pa aimap.subscriptions.lock
and
.Pa aimap.subscriptions.tmp
beside it.
While it is absent, every mailbox is subscribed.
If it cannot be read, or holds a name that is not a valid mailbox name,
.Li LSUB
and
.Li LIST
(SUBSCRIBED)
answer
.Li NO .
.It Pa aimap.uidvalidity
Highest
.Li UIDVALIDITY
issued, at the root of each maildir.
If it cannot be read or written, a value already issued may be issued
again.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aimap.conf 5 ,
.Xr smtpd 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%A A. Melnikov
.%A B. Leiba
.%D August 2021
.%R RFC 9051
.%T Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - Version 4rev2
.Re
.Pp
.Rs
.%A A. Melnikov
.%A D. Cridland
.%A C. Newman
.%D May 2014
.%R RFC 7162
.%T IMAP Extensions: Quick Flag Changes Resynchronization (CONDSTORE) and Quick Mailbox Resynchronization (QRESYNC)
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
is a from-scratch IMAP server written by Claude AI, in
the
.Ox
privilege-separation tradition of
.Xr smtpd 8 .
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
advertises IMAP4rev2 but does not yet implement all of it:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Header text in a character set other than UTF-8 is not converted.
In
.Li ENVELOPE
and
.Li BODYSTRUCTURE ,
each byte that is not part of a UTF-8 character is sent as its
.Xr vis 3
octal escape, such as
.Ql \e351 .
.It
.Li SEARCH
undoes transfer encodings and encoded words but does not convert
character sets, so text that is neither US-ASCII nor UTF-8 matches only
byte for byte.
.It
.Li BODYSTRUCTURE
carries no extension data, such as the content disposition, and its
parameters are not decoded as RFC 2231 describes.
.It
PLAIN is the only SASL mechanism, and its authorization identity is
ignored.
.It
.Li INTERNALDATE
is always given in UTC.
.El
.Pp
Other commands, options and
.Li FETCH
items it does not support are refused with
.Li BAD
or
.Li NO .
.Pp
These limits are fixed:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A message whose header is longer than 8192 bytes cannot be fetched with
.Li BODY[HEADER] ,
.Li HEADER.FIELDS
or
.Li ENVELOPE ,
and the
.Li FETCH
completes
.Li NO .
.It
.Li ENVELOPE
fails the same way when it would exceed 8192 bytes, and so does a part's
.Li HEADER.FIELDS
section.
.It
.Li BODYSTRUCTURE
fails the same way when it would exceed 12000 bytes, or when the message
has more than 64 MIME parts, parts nested more than ten levels below the
top, or a multipart part with no boundary.
.It
A sequence set may hold at most 500 comma-separated ranges.
.It
A part number in a
.Li FETCH
section may have at most ten levels and 39 characters;
an item with a deeper or longer one is left out, and the
.Li FETCH
completes
.Li NO
with
.Li [LIMIT] .
.It
A
.Li FETCH
may name at most 16 body, header and binary sections;
those past the sixteenth are left out, and the
.Li FETCH
completes
.Li NO
with
.Li [LIMIT] .
.It
A command, with its literals, may not exceed 8191 bytes, except for the
message given to
.Li APPEND .
.It
At most eight commands may wait behind one that is still running.
One more closes the connection.
.It
A message's keywords, joined by commas, may not exceed 255 bytes;
a
.Li STORE
that would exceed this fails.
.It
.Li APPEND
refuses a date before 1970.
.It
Mailboxes have no hierarchy: no mailbox name may contain
.Sq / .
.Li LIST
and
.Li NAMESPACE
report
.Sq /
as the hierarchy delimiter, every mailbox is listed
.Li \eNoInferiors ,
and
.Li CREATE
ignores one trailing
.Sq / .
.El
.Pp
.Li IDLE
polls the selected mailbox, so new mail is reported within one
.Ic idle poll
interval.
Files placed directly in
.Pa cur
are not noticed during
.Li IDLE
until something else changes the mailbox.
.Pp
One process serves all sessions of an account, so a
.Li STORE ,
.Li EXPUNGE ,
.Li COPY
or
.Li MOVE
over a very large mailbox delays the account's other sessions.
.Pp
If the process holding the TLS private key exits,
.Nm
shuts down, ending every session, and exits with status 1.
.Pp
.Pa tmp ,
.Pa new ,
.Pa cur
and the maildir files under
.Sx FILES
cannot be used as mailbox names.
.Pp
A mailbox name must be in Unicode Normalization Form C and use only
characters assigned in Unicode 16.0.0.
A mailbox directory whose name does not, such as one made by an
earlier version of
.Nm ,
is skipped by
.Li LIST ,
with a warning in the log, and must be renamed by hand.
